Template: fips-initramfs/peer-npub
Type: string
Default:
Description: Bootstrap peer npub:
 The node in the initramfs reaches the mesh through static peers. It cannot
 use Nostr rendezvous, which needs DNS, a plausible clock and relay
 reachability, none of which hold that early in a boot.
 .
 Give the npub of a peer this machine should dial at boot. It should be an
 always-on node with a public endpoint.
 .
 Leave this blank to use the FIPS public test mesh, which accepts inbound
 peering from any npub. That is fine for trying the package out. Replace it
 for a machine you rely on: if every configured peer has moved, this machine
 becomes unreachable with no signal as to why, and the only recovery is
 physical access to the console.
 .
 This is asked only when the configuration file does not already exist. It
 never overwrites one you have edited.

Template: fips-initramfs/peer-transport
Type: string
Default: udp
Description: Bootstrap peer transport:
 The FIPS transport used to dial the peer, normally udp. Other values are
 tcp, tor and ethernet, though only udp and tcp make sense this early in a
 boot.

Template: fips-initramfs/peer-address
Type: string
Default:
Description: Bootstrap peer address:
 Where to dial the peer, as host:port. Either a hostname or a literal
 address will do.
 .
 A hostname needs a resolver, which the initramfs gets from its DHCP lease.
 A literal address needs none. If you have both, put them in the
 configuration file afterwards as two entries with different priorities, the
 way the shipped defaults do.
 .
 Ignored if no npub was given above.

Template: fips-initramfs/ssh-key
Type: string
Default:
Description: SSH public key that may unlock this machine:
 Dropbear admits one or more public keys into the initramfs, and a session
 opened with one of them unlocks the encrypted root. Without a key there is
 no remote unlock at all, and this machine can only be unlocked at its
 console.
 .
 Paste one public key, the whole line, as it appears in a .pub file. For
 example: ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... you@host
 .
 It is written to /etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys, which belongs to
 no package and is yours to edit afterwards. Add further keys there.
 .
 This is asked only when that file names no key yet. An existing key is
 never overwritten.
